    {
      "slug": "story",
      "title": "Our Story",
      "role": "editorial",
      "goal": "Tell the Lava Mouth origin story in a way that makes the brand feel human, earned, and worth rooting for. This is the page that converts casual buyers into loyal fans. The scroll should feel like reading a great short story \u2014 deliberate, warm, and surprising.",
      "meta_description": "How a backyard obsession with peppers turned into Lava Mouth \u2014 the small-batch hot sauce brand built on flavor, fire, and stubbornness.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "id": "story-section-1",
          "type": "page_header",
          "intent": "Open the story with a cinematic, emotionally charged statement that signals this page is different \u2014 slower, more personal, worth reading. A deliberate stillness after the energy of the rest of the site.",
          "eyebrow": "The Origin",
          "headline": "It Started With a Pepper Plant and a Dare",
          "subheadline": "The honest, slightly embarrassing story of how Lava Mouth went from a backyard garden to a bottle on your shelf.",
          "body": null,
          "items": [],
          "ctas": [],
          "asset_ideas": []
        },
        {
          "id": "story-section-2",
          "type": "story_block",
          "intent": "The founding story told in full \u2014 personal, funny, and genuine. This section should feel like the founder is talking directly to the reader. Slow, warm, and human.",
          "eyebrow": "Chapter One",
          "headline": "A Garden, a Blender, and a Very Unhappy Neighbor",
          "subheadline": null,
          "body": "In 2018, founder Dani Reyes planted a single row of ghost peppers in her Chicago apartment's community garden plot. By August, she had more peppers than she knew what to do with. By September, she had a blender, a dozen mason jars, and a sauce that her neighbors described as 'aggressively good' and 'slightly dangerous.' By December, she was selling out of a folding table at the Logan Square Farmers Market every Saturday. Nobody was more surprised than Dani.\n\nLava Mouth wasn't planned. It was grown. The early sauces were made in a rented commercial kitchen from midnight to 4am, labeled by hand at the kitchen table, and sold from the back of a hatchback. Every batch was an experiment. Most were brilliant. A few were spectacular failures that have been sworn to secrecy.\n\nThe name came from Dani's nephew, who tried the original ghost pepper sauce at age nine and announced \u2014 with tears streaming down his face \u2014 that his mouth felt like lava. He asked for more. The name stuck.",
          "items": [],
          "ctas": [],
          "asset_ideas": [
            {
              "id": "story-section-2-asset-1",
              "kind": "illustration",
              "subject": "A Memphis-style illustrated scene of a small community garden plot with a single row of chili pepper plants growing vigorously, surrounded by bold geometric shapes and a tiny folding market table in the background",
              "purpose": "Visual storytelling anchor for the founding narrative \u2014 sets the humble, scrappy origin scene in the brand's playful visual language",
              "quantity": 1,
              "used_by": [
                "story-section-2"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "story-section-3",
          "type": "timeline",
          "intent": "Walk visitors through the brand's growth milestones in a way that feels like a victory lap \u2014 each moment building momentum and proving this is a real, growing brand with a real history. Let each milestone land with a little pop of energy.",
          "eyebrow": "The Journey",
          "headline": "From Mason Jars to Shelves Nationwide",
          "subheadline": null,
          "body": null,
          "items": [
            {
              "title": "2018 \u2014 The Garden",
              "description": "First ghost pepper harvest. First batch. First farmers market table. First completely accidental business.",
              "metadata": {}
            },
            {
              "title": "2019 \u2014 The Kitchen",
              "description": "Moved to a licensed commercial kitchen. Launched Serrano Verde and Smoky Diablo. First 10 local stockists.",
              "metadata": {}
            },
            {
              "title": "2020 \u2014 The Pivot",
              "description": "Farmers markets closed. Online sales launched. Discovered that the internet also loves hot sauce, possibly more than anyone expected.",
              "metadata": {}
            },
            {
              "title": "2021 \u2014 The Shelf",
              "description": "First regional grocery placement. 25 stockists. Mango Inferno launched and immediately became the best-selling sauce.",
              "metadata": {}
            },
            {
              "title": "2022 \u2014 The Team",
              "description": "Hired first two full-time employees. Moved to a dedicated production facility. Still making every batch by hand.",
              "metadata": {}
            },
            {
              "title": "2023\u2013Now \u2014 The Movement",
              "description": "85+ stockists. 40,000+ bottles sold. Six sauces in the permanent lineup. Still small-batch. Always will be.",
              "metadata": {}
            }
          ],
          "ctas": [],
          "asset_ideas": []
        },
        {
          "id": "story-section-4",
          "type": "story_block",
          "intent": "Articulate the brand's philosophy and values \u2014 what Lava Mouth stands for beyond just hot sauce. This section should feel like a manifesto, warm and confident, not corporate.",
          "eyebrow": "What We Believe",
          "headline": "Flavor First. Heat Second. Always Small-Batch.",
          "subheadline": null,
          "body": "A lot of hot sauce brands are in the business of pain. We're in the business of flavor. The heat is real \u2014 sometimes brutally so \u2014 but every Lava Mouth sauce is built around a flavor profile first. The pepper comes second. That's the rule Dani set in 2018 and it hasn't changed.\n\nSmall-batch isn't a marketing phrase for us. It's a hard cap. Every run is 500 bottles or fewer. That means we can adjust recipes based on how the peppers are tasting that season. It means we can try new things without betting the whole company on them. It means every bottle you open is the result of someone's actual attention, not a formula running on autopilot.\n\nWe source our peppers from three family farms \u2014 two in New Mexico, one in the Yucat\u00e1n. We've worked with the same growers since year one. When the harvest is good, the sauce is extraordinary. When it's a tough season, we adjust. That's the deal.",
          "items": [],
          "ctas": [],
          "asset_ideas": [
            {
              "id": "story-section-4-asset-1",
              "kind": "illustration",
              "subject": "A Memphis-style illustrated map of the United States with two bold markers in New Mexico and one in the Yucat\u00e1n region of Mexico, connected by dotted lines to a central production point in Chicago, surrounded by small chili pepper icons",
              "purpose": "Visual anchor for the sourcing philosophy \u2014 makes the farm-to-bottle story tangible and geographic",
              "quantity": 1,
              "used_by": [
                "story-section-4"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "story-section-5",
          "type": "cta",
          "intent": "Close the story page with a warm, personal invitation \u2014 the natural next step after falling in love with the brand. The energy here is like a handshake at the end of a great conversation.",
          "eyebrow": "Now You Know Us",
